WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.490
Quantization lowers numerical precision so a model uses less memory and often

00:00:04.490 --> 00:00:06.685
runs more practically on local hardware.

00:00:06.685 --> 00:00:10.344
Labels such as Q2 through Q8 describe broad precision levels,

00:00:10.344 --> 00:00:12.905
but exact schemes and model behavior differ.

00:00:12.905 --> 00:00:18.393
Lower-bit quantization generally reduces file and memory size while increasing the chance of quality loss.

00:00:18.393 --> 00:00:23.515
Higher precision uses more resources and is not automatically better for every task, runtime,

00:00:23.515 --> 00:00:24.247
or machine.

00:00:24.247 --> 00:00:25.710
Test the actual file.

00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:30.100
G G U F is a common local model format for llama.

00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:30.832
cpp-compatible runtimes.

00:00:30.832 --> 00:00:34.491
Compatibility depends on architecture, runtime version, tokenizer, prompt template, metadata,

00:00:34.491 --> 00:00:37.417
and acceleration backend, not only the file extension.

00:00:37.417 --> 00:00:41.076
Memory fit includes model weights, key-value cache, context, batch settings,

00:00:41.076 --> 00:00:44.369
concurrent sessions, runtime overhead, display use, and other applications.

00:00:44.369 --> 00:00:48.393
File size is therefore not a complete VRAM or unified-memory estimate.

00:00:48.393 --> 00:00:52.784
Some runtimes allow different cache precision or memory strategies, creating another quality,

00:00:52.784 --> 00:00:54.247
compatibility, and capacity tradeoff.

00:00:54.247 --> 00:00:58.637
Treat those settings as part of the tested configuration instead of assuming

00:00:58.637 --> 00:01:00.832
model quantization tells the whole story.

00:01:00.832 --> 00:01:06.686
Partial GPU offload can run some layers on the accelerator and leave others on the CPU.

00:01:06.686 --> 00:01:09.247
This may make a larger model load,

00:01:09.247 --> 00:01:12.174
but transfer and CPU work can reduce speed.

00:01:12.174 --> 00:01:15.467
A technically successful load is not an operational success.

00:01:15.467 --> 00:01:19.125
Avoid configurations that consume nearly all available memory, swap heavily,

00:01:19.125 --> 00:01:22.418
crash on long prompts, or throttle under sustained work.

00:01:22.418 --> 00:01:25.345
Compare several quantizations and sizes with realistic context,

00:01:25.345 --> 00:01:27.540
then preserve a margin for stability.
